I'll help you convert your Spring Security WebMVC OAuth2/session configuration to WebFlux. Let me search for the most current WebFlux equivalents since these APIs evolve over time.

I have enough information to provide a comprehensive mapping. Here's how to convert your WebMVC Spring Security configuration to the WebFlux (reactive) version using `ServerHttpSecurity`.

## Key Conceptual Changes

In WebFlux, you build a `SecurityWebFilterChain` from `ServerHttpSecurity` instead of building an `HttpSecurity`. The configurers become **"Spec"** classes, and most MVC handlers get a `Server*` reactive equivalent (returning `Mono<Void>` instead of being void/blocking).

SecurityWebFilterChain is the governing chain of WebFilters that allows you to lock down reactive WebFlux applications, and with @EnableWebFluxSecurity turned on, you build this object by issuing commands to the ServerHttpSecurity DSL object.

