## 2. LogoutConfigurer → `ServerHttpSecurity.logout()`

The reactive `LogoutSpec` is more streamlined. Logout is configured via .logout(...) where you set logoutHandler, logoutUrl (e.g. POST /signout), and logoutSuccessHandler.

| WebMVC (`LogoutConfigurer`) | WebFlux (`LogoutSpec`) |
|---|---|
| `logoutUrl(...)` | `logoutUrl(...)` |
| `addLogoutHandler(...)` | `logoutHandler(ServerLogoutHandler)` |
| `invalidateHttpSession(true)` | Use `WebSessionServerLogoutHandler` (default — invalidates the WebSession) |
| `clearAuthentication(true)` | `SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler` (default clears context) |
| `deleteCookies(...)` | Add a custom/cookie-clearing `ServerLogoutHandler` |
| `logoutSuccessUrl(...)` | `logoutSuccessHandler(new RedirectServerLogoutSuccessHandler(...))` |

```java
http.logout(logout -> logout
    // logoutUrl() equivalent
    .logoutUrl("/logout")
    // addLogoutHandler() equivalent — combine multiple handlers
    .logoutHandler(new DelegatingServerLogoutHandler(
        new WebSessionServerLogoutHandler(),          // invalidateHttpSession + session
        new SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler(),     // clearAuthentication
        new HeaderWriterServerLogoutHandler(           // deleteCookies (clear cookie)
            new ClearSiteDataHeaderWriter(Directive.COOKIES))
    ))
    // logoutSuccessUrl() equivalent
    .logoutSuccessHandler(redirectLogoutSuccessHandler("/bye"))
);
```

Note the reactive defaults for the handlers you listed:
- **`invalidateHttpSession`** — there are no servlet `HttpSession`s in WebFlux; the reactive equivalent is `WebSessionServerLogoutHandler`, which invalidates the `WebSession` and is part of the default logout handling.
- **`clearAuthentication`** — handled by the default `SecurityContextServerLogoutHandler`.
- **`deleteCookies`** — there's no direct `deleteCookies(...)` DSL method; use a `ServerLogoutHandler` (e.g. a `HeaderWriterServerLogoutHandler` with `ClearSiteDataHeaderWriter`, or a small custom handler that calls `exchange.getResponse().addCookie(...)` with maxAge 0).

For redirect-on-success, as the reactive docs note: for logout successes you send the user to an endpoint by configuring the RedirectServerLogoutSuccessHandler in a separate method since its constructor doesn't support parameters.

```java
private RedirectServerLogoutSuccessHandler redirectLogoutSuccessHandler(String url) {
    RedirectServerLogoutSuccessHandler handler = new RedirectServerLogoutSuccessHandler();
    handler.setLogoutSuccessUrl(URI.create(url));
    return handler;
}
```

