## 3. SessionManagementConfigurer → `ServerHttpSecurity.sessionManagement()`

This is the area with the biggest differences, because WebFlux uses `WebSession` (reactive) rather than the servlet `HttpSession`, and several MVC concepts don't have a 1:1 mapping.

Session Management is configured via .sessionManagement(...), for example configuring concurrentSessions with maxSessions(1), maxSessionsPreventsLogin(true), and a sessionRegistry, using a ReactiveSessionRegistry.

| WebMVC (`SessionManagementConfigurer`) | WebFlux (`SessionManagementSpec`) |
|---|---|
| `sessionCreationPolicy(...)` | No direct equivalent — control via a custom `ServerSecurityContextRepository` (e.g. `NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository` for stateless, set on `http.securityContextRepository(...)`) |
| `sessionConcurrency(...)` | `concurrentSessions(...)` with `maxSessions`, `maxSessionsPreventsLogin`, `sessionRegistry(ReactiveSessionRegistry)` |
| `invalidSessionStrategy(...)` | `concurrentSessions(...).maximumSessionsExceededHandler(...)` / related handlers |
| `sessionFixation()` | Handled automatically by the reactive `WebSession` infrastructure (no explicit DSL needed) |

```java
http.sessionManagement(session -> session
    // sessionConcurrency() equivalent
    .concurrentSessions(concurrency -> concurrency
        .maximumSessions(SessionLimit.of(1))
        .maximumSessionsExceededHandler(
            new PreventLoginServerMaximumSessionsExceededHandler())  // maxSessionsPreventsLogin(true)
    )
);
```

For **`sessionCreationPolicy(STATELESS)`** (the most common use), set a no-op security context repository on `ServerHttpSecurity` directly rather than inside `sessionManagement`:

```java
http.securityContextRepository(NoOpServerSecurityContextRepository.getInstance());
```

A `ReactiveSessionRegistry` bean is required for concurrency control. **Session fixation** protection is built into the WebSession handling and has no explicit reactive DSL method.

