## 4. OAuth2ClientConfigurer.authorizationCodeGrant() → `ServerHttpSecurity.oauth2Client()`

You enable it with .oauth2Client(withDefaults()), after which you can leverage Spring Security's WebClient or @RegisteredOAuth2AuthorizedClient support to obtain and use the access token.

The MVC `authorizationCodeGrant()` sub-configurer maps onto the `OAuth2ClientSpec`, where you set the `authenticationManager` (with the authorization-code reactive auth manager) and the authorization request repository/resolver:

```java
http.oauth2Client(oauth2Client -> oauth2Client
    .authenticationManager(
        new OAuth2AuthorizationCodeReactiveAuthenticationManager(tokenResponseClient))
    // authorizationCodeGrant().authorizationRequestRepository(...) equivalent:
    // .authorizationRequestRepository(...)
    // .authorizationRequestResolver(...)
);
```

This is shown in the combined example: oauth2Client is configured with .authenticationManager(new OAuth2AuthorizationCodeReactiveAuthenticationManager(accessTokenResponseClient)).

